The End-to-End Deployment Flow
[Excel Checklist] ➔ [CyberArk] ➔ [Secure VM Jumpbox] ➔ [Network Drive / PPD] ➔ [Jenkins / Ansible] ➔ [Target Linux Server] ➔ [Marketplace]
Step 1: Read the Blueprint (The Start)
Where to go: Open the Evidence Excel Sheet (the Operational Runbook).

What to get: Get the specific release version number (e.g., 26.6.0-RELEASE-SNAPSHOT), the target server names, and the exact step-by-step checklist of instructions.

Connection made: This step connects human instructions to the software tools. You follow this line-by-line to know what tool to open next.

Step 2: Establish Security Clearance
Where to go: Log into CyberArk.

What to get: Request administrative access or a temporary security token for the target application environment.

Connection made: CyberArk links your personal corporate identity to the highly privileged root or system application credentials required to modify backend environments.

Step 3: Enter the Isolated Sandbox
Where to go: Click the "computer symbol" to launch your secure Virtual Machine (VM) / Citrix Jumpbox.

What to get: An isolated, secure network workspace that sits inside the bank's internal firewall.

Connection made: Your local laptop establishes a secure remote-desktop connection to this VM. From this point forward, all further actions, tool openings, and connections happen entirely inside this secure VM, not on your physical computer.

Step 4: Retrieve the Deployment Code Assets
Where to go: Navigate to the designated secure network file share / PPD (Pre-Production) Drive.

What to get: Locate the project folder (Eurosycomore). Inside, grab the packaged deployment files containing the new database compliance scripts (.sql) and data transformation rules (WTX maps).

Where to put it: These files are staged here so the automation pipeline can access them.

Step 5: Trigger the Automation Pipeline
Where to go: Open CloudBees Jenkins in the web browser (inside the VM).

What to get: Find the specific pipeline build job (e.g., esy-deploy-aap).

Connection made: You input the parameters (the version number and environment targets from Step 1) and hit run. Jenkins links directly to Red Hat Ansible by triggering an Ansible playbook job (e.g., j-APO2861-1412-esy-esy-deploy) and passing it those parameters.

Step 6: Live Execution on the Engine Room
Where to go: Open SuperPutty (or PowerShell) to SSH directly into the target Linux server host (e.g., s01v19925672).

What to get: Real-time visibility into the Linux command line (/home/sybase/tmp/...).

Connection made: Ansible logs into this target Linux server via SSH using the credentials authorized back in Step 2.

What happens here:

Ansible commands the server to Stop active WTX data processing services so files don't collide.

Ansible copies the .sql and WTX files from the staged drive (Step 4) onto the server.

The server executes the SQL scripts to update the database tables, skipping unchanged files and logging successes.

Ansible commands the server to Start the services back up.

Step 7: Final Verification (The End)
Where to go: Open the internal Organization Marketplace Catalogue via the browser.

What to get: Look at the system registry dashboard for Eurosycomore.

Connection made: The marketplace pulls live configuration data directly from the server environment. You verify that the displayed version has successfully changed to the new deployment version.

Where to put the results: Take screenshots of the successful SuperPutty completion log and the updated Marketplace version, and put them back into the Evidence Excel Sheet from Step 1 to mark the deployment as complete and audited.